.TH MD5SUM 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha384sum, sha3sum, sha512sum \-
print or check digests
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B md5sum
.RB [ \-b " | " \-t ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B md5sum \-c
.RB [ \-sw ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B sha3sum
.RB [ \-b " | " \-t ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR bits ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B sha3sum \-c
.RB [ \-sw ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR bits ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These commands print the message digest of each
.IR file ,
or with
.B \-c
check files against digests listed in other files.
They are one program, and differ only in the algorithm:
.TP
.B md5sum
MD5, a 128-bit digest.
.TP
.B sha1sum
SHA-1, 160 bits.
.TP
.B sha256sum
SHA-256, 256 bits.
.TP
.B sha384sum
SHA-384, 384 bits.
.TP
.B sha512sum
SHA-512, 512 bits.
.TP
.B sha3sum
SHA-3, 224 bits unless
.B \-a
chooses another size.
.PP
.BR sha1sum ,
.BR sha256sum ,
.BR sha384sum ,
and
.B sha512sum
take the same options and operands as
.BR md5sum .
Only
.B sha3sum
takes
.BR \-a .
.PP
With no
.IR file ,
or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
they read standard input.
Every file is read as binary.
.SS Printing digests
For each
.IR file ,
the command writes a line of the digest in lowercase hex digits, two
spaces, and the name exactly as given.
Standard input is named
.BR \- .
With
.BR \-b ,
the second space is an asterisk.
A file that cannot be opened or read is reported on standard error and
gets no line, and the remaining files are still processed.
.SS Checking digests
With
.BR \-c ,
each
.I file
is a list of digests, such as the output of the same command, and the
command hashes every file that a list names and compares the digests.
A list named
.BR \- ,
or no list, is read from standard input.
Names in a list are relative to the current directory, not to the list.
.PP
A list line is a digest, a space, optionally one more space or an
asterisk, and a name, which is the rest of the line, including any
spaces.
A CR before the LF is dropped.
The digest is everything before the first space.
It is compared with the computed digest without regard to case.
Its length and digits are not checked, so a line with a digest of
another algorithm is not invalid: its file is hashed, and the line
fails.
A name of
.B \-
hashes standard input.
A line with no space, such as a blank line, is invalid and counts as
failed.
Nothing is skipped: a comment line is read as a digest and a name, and
fails.
.PP
For each valid line, the command writes
.IB name ": OK"
or
.IB name ": FAILED"
on standard output.
A file that cannot be opened or read is reported on standard error, and
also counts as
.BR FAILED .
After each list in which any line failed, it writes to standard error
a line such as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
md5sum: WARNING: 1 of 2 computed checksums did NOT match
.EE
.RE
.PP
which begins with the name of the command.
The first count is the lines of that list that failed, and the second
is all its lines, invalid lines included.
A list with no lines is reported as
.IB list ": no checksum lines found"
on standard error, and counts as a failure.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-cs ,
and may follow the operands.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
The argument of
.B \-a
may be attached, as in
.BR \-a256 ,
or be the next argument.
.TP
.B \-c
Check the digests listed in the
.I file
operands, as described under
.BR "Checking digests" .
.TP
.B \-s
Do not write the
.BR OK ,
.BR FAILED ,
and
.B WARNING
lines, so that only the exit status tells whether every digest matched.
Other errors are still written to standard error: files that cannot be
opened, lists with no lines, and with
.BR \-w ,
invalid lines.
Requires
.BR \-c :
without it, the command writes its usage message to standard error and
exits with status 1.
.TP
.B \-w
Warn about each invalid line by writing
.B invalid format
to standard error.
The message does not name the line.
Requires
.BR \-c ,
like
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BI "\-a " bits
Set the size of the digest in bits, which is written as
.IR bits /4
hex digits.
The other commands reject
.B \-a
as an unknown option.
The default is 224.
Sizes 224, 256, 384, and 512 give SHA3-224, SHA3-256, SHA3-384, and
SHA3-512 as standardized by FIPS 202.
Any other multiple of 32 from 32 to 768 is accepted, and gives a
nonstandard digest.
Any other number ends the command with status 1 and an error such as
.B sha3sum: bad \-a100
when the first file is opened, after any earlier files that could not
be opened are reported.
A value that is not a plain decimal number, such as
.B \-5
or
.BR 0x100 ,
is an error at once.
A list does not record its size, so give the same
.B \-a
when checking.
.TP
.B \-b
Binary mode: write an asterisk instead of the second space before each
name.
It affects only the output, since every file is read as binary.
Ignored with
.BR \-c .
A later
.B \-t
cancels it.
.TP
.B \-t
Text mode, the default: write two spaces before each name.
A later
.B \-b
cancels it, and
.B \-bt
is the same as
.BR \-t .
Ignored with
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-b
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when the program starts, even in
quoted operands, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: every file was hashed, or with
.B \-c
every listed file matched.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a file that could not be opened or read, a bad
.BR \-a ,
and with
.BR \-c ,
a digest that did not match, an invalid line, a list with no lines, or a
list that could not be opened.
.PP
A list that cannot be opened, and a bad
.BR \-a ,
end the command at once, and later operands and list lines are not
processed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the MD5 digest of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf abc >abc.txt
$ md5sum abc.txt
900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72  abc.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Standard input is named
.BR \- :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ md5sum <abc.txt
900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72  -
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print only the SHA-256 digest of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sha256sum <release.zip | cut \-d" " \-f1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Record digests, then check them later:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sha256sum *.zip >SHA256SUMS
$ sha256sum \-c SHA256SUMS
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a file against a published digest:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo "$digest  release.zip" | sha256sum \-c
release.zip: OK
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check without the OK and FAILED lines, and report failure:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sha256sum \-cs SHA256SUMS || echo corrupt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Record SHA3-256 digests of a tree, then check them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src \-type f \-exec sha3sum \-a 256 {} + >tree.sha3
$ sha3sum \-a 256 \-c tree.sha3
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU coreutils:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-s
is GNU's
.BR \-\-status .
There are no long options besides
.BR \-\-help ,
and no equivalents of GNU
.BR \-\-quiet ,
.BR \-\-ignore\-missing ,
.BR \-\-strict ,
.BR \-\-tag ,
or
.BR \-z .
.IP \(bu 2
An invalid line in a list makes the exit status 1, where GNU skips it
unless given
.BR \-\-strict .
.IP \(bu 2
Names are neither escaped when written nor unescaped when read.
GNU starts the line with a backslash and escapes names that contain a
backslash or a newline.
.PP
Standard input from a file or pipe is read as binary, like files, so a
digest covers the exact bytes.
A text file with CR LF line endings has a different digest from a copy
with LF line endings, and
.B \-t
does not change that.
Lists may have either line ending.
Output lines end with LF.
.PP
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings, which are part
of the digest.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
.PP
Standard input is not closed after it is read.
A second
.B \-
operand hashes what remains, which at end of file is nothing.
.PP
A
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
It is printed exactly as given, so a list made with backslashes can be
checked as it is.
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
A directory fails with
.BR "Permission denied" ,
or with
.B Is a directory
when it is a list for
.BR \-c .
.PP
When standard output is a console, names are converted for display from
the ANSI code page to the console's output code page.
.PP
MD5 and SHA-1 detect accidental damage only, not deliberate tampering.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cksum (1),
.BR cut (1),
.BR find (1)
